This is an introduction to investing in startup technology enterprises:

**1. Conditions for Startup Technology Enterprises**:
- Registered as a resident enterprise under audit-based tax collection within mainland China (excluding Hong Kong, Macau, and Taiwan);
- At the time of investment, the number of employees does not exceed 300, with at least 30% holding a bachelor's degree or higher, and total assets and annual sales revenue both do not exceed 50 million yuan;
- Established for no more than 5 years at the time of investment;
- Not publicly listed at the time of investment and within 2 years after receiving the investment;
- At the time of investment and the following year, R&D expenses account for at least 20% of total cost expenses.

**2. Tax Incentives for Investment**:
- Corporate venture capital enterprises holding investments for at least 2 years may deduct 70% of the investment amount from taxable income in the year when the equity has been held for 2 years, with any remaining amount carried forward;
- Limited partnership venture capital enterprises holding investments for at least 2 years allow corporate partners and individual partners to respectively deduct 70% of the investment amount from their share of income or operating income from the partnership venture capital enterprise, with any remaining amount carried forward;
- Angel investors holding investments for at least 2 years may deduct 70% of the investment amount from taxable income arising from the transfer of equity in the startup technology enterprise, with any remaining amount carried forward to offset taxable income from future transfers of equity in the same enterprise. For investments in multiple startup technology enterprises, any undeducted 70% of the investment amount for enterprises undergoing deregistration and liquidation may be deducted from taxable income arising from the transfer of equity in other startup technology enterprises within 36 months from the date of deregistration and liquidation.

This is a guide on how to achieve work-life balance:

**1. Current Situation**: Work takes up the largest portion of time aside from sleep. Without proper planning, it can easily encroach on family, leisure, and other aspects of life.

**2. Signs of Imbalance**:
+ Sleep is squeezed out by work, and work efficiency continuously declines;
+ Work invades personal life, and health issues arise;
+ Emotions become irritable or numb, and fatigue is hard to alleviate;
+ Monday anxiety becomes the norm, and interpersonal relationships gradually become distant.

**3. Improvement Suggestions**:
+ Set clear boundaries to separate work and personal time;
+ Prioritize high-value tasks using the four-quadrant method;
+ Take regular short breaks, following the "Pomodoro Technique";
+ Allocate tasks reasonably, seek help from colleagues at work, and share responsibilities with family members at home;
+ Learn to say no gently to avoid overcommitting;
+ Exercise daily, reserving 30 minutes for physical activity;
+ Plan the week ahead, reviewing the next week's schedule on Sunday night;
+ Engage in hobbies and recharge by regularly doing things you enjoy.

